                        "reasoning": "The application uses `multipartFile.getOriginalFilename()` directly in `Path.resolve()`. An attacker can provide a filename containing path traversal sequences (e.g., '../../evil.sh'). While the code resolves this against `destinationDir` (which is scoped by username), `Path.resolve()` in Java can return an absolute path if the argument is absolute, or traverse out of the intended directory if the filename contains '..'. This allows an attacker to overwrite files outside of their designated user directory if the underlying filesystem or OS allows it.",
